{
  "_comment": "Copy to /etc/eyeguard/phone.json and fill in the CAPS values.",

  "supabase_url": "https://ucgldleacehxjjwwqomk.supabase.co",
  "_key": "Admin-trust pivot (2026-08-24): the public anon/publishable key -- safe to commit, same one the Mac and every other client in this project uses. There is no secret key on the router anymore; every write goes through either flags' anon-scoped insert-only RLS policy or a SECURITY DEFINER RPC that stamps server-side.",
  "api_key": "sb_publishable_y9pDf3aBNY4Le5u9tbUXzg_qCRvMn-b",

  "_home": "The phone's LAN identity when on home wifi. Set a Reserved IP for it in the router's Clients page first, and turn OFF Private Wi-Fi Address on the phone for this network -- otherwise the IP/MAC drift and capture silently stops matching.",
  "home_ip": "192.168.8.153",
  "home_interface": "br-lan",

  "_wg": "The phone's identity on the WireGuard tunnel (away from home). Also the sleep-aware liveness signal: set Persistent Keepalive = 25 on the phone's peer (router GUI) so a sleeping-but-connected phone still reads alive; only a truly-down tunnel goes dark. Find the interface name with `wg show` on the router (often NOT wg0 -- GL.iNet's Flint 2 names it e.g. wgserver). CONFIRMED LIVE MISTAKE (2026-08-24 redeploy, found+fixed 2026-08-29): the Mac ALSO has its own WireGuard peer on this same interface (see privacy-network-stack notes -- 'WIREGUARD ALWAYS-ON EXCEPT HOME, BOTH DEVICES') -- wg_peer/wg_ip here were wired to the MAC's peer/IP instead of the phone's after a redeploy, so wg_rx_bytes() spent days tracking the Mac's tunnel traffic as the phone's liveness signal, causing real phone-dark false alarms that raising dark_buffer_seconds alone could never fix (the watched signal was just wrong, not noisy). The router's own peer-drift tamper check (router_snapshot()'s wg_peers diff) does NOT catch this class of bug -- it only detects a peer being ADDED or REMOVED from the interface, not this config pointing at the wrong EXISTING one. Before setting wg_peer/wg_ip, cross-check against `awg show wgserver`'s own `allowed ips` field for the value you're about to enter -- don't just copy whatever pubkey a redeploy script or note happens to have on hand.",
  "wg_interface": "wgserver",
  "wg_ip": "10.1.0.3",
  "wg_peer": "PHONE_WIREGUARD_PUBLIC_KEY",

  "_dark_buffer": "Confirmed live (2026-08-27): 60s left almost no safety margin. heartbeat_loop() polls every heartbeat_seconds (20s) and, while away, the only liveness signal is the WireGuard rx-byte counter driven by the 25s Persistent Keepalive -- 20 and 25 beat against each other (gcd=5), so periodically a poll lands just before a keepalive arrives and the gap since the last confirmed rx-increase stretches to ~45s even under ideal conditions. With dark_buffer_seconds=60 that leaves ~15s of margin against normal jitter (a slow `awg show` call, a delayed keepalive, one dropped packet) -- not enough, and it was flapping alive/dark/alive purely from timing noise while the phone was just asleep. Each flap inserts a real row into the flags table (not a debounced status update), so this was polluting the actual review queue, not just email. 150s gives real margin (heartbeat+keepalive worst case ~45s vs a 150s cutoff) while still catching a genuinely-dead phone within about the same couple-minute ballpark as the Mac-side dark-detection thresholds elsewhere in this system.",
  "dark_buffer_seconds": 150,
  "_transition_grace": "⚠️ REDUCES COVERAGE for one narrow case (see the PR that added this, and eyeguard-phone.py's heartbeat_loop() docstring for the full evidence/reasoning). Confirmed live: 17 phone-dark flags 2026-09-22..27, every one reading 'silent for 168s' or 'silent for 169s' -- the ~168-169s it genuinely takes for the on-demand WireGuard tunnel to reconnect after leaving home Wi-Fi (cellular acquisition + on-demand VPN evaluation + the handshake itself), invisible to every liveness signal this router has. This grants dark_buffer_seconds + transition_grace_seconds (not dark_buffer_seconds alone) as the firing threshold, but ONLY while the most recently confirmed-alive signal was specifically a HOME one and nothing has reconfirmed home OR away since -- a phone already away when it goes dark is completely unaffected (still the tight dark_buffer_seconds). Cost: the router cannot tell 'mid-transition, will reconnect shortly' apart from 'switched off at the exact moment of leaving home' -- both look identical from here -- so a genuine dark event that begins AT HOME is delayed by up to this many extra seconds. Every time the grace actually suppresses a would-have-fired alert, it's logged, not silent.",
  "transition_grace_seconds": 40,
  "green_repeat_seconds": 900,
  "heartbeat_seconds": 20,
  "_report_seconds": "How often the local heartbeat_seconds poll actually REPORTS to Supabase. Split from heartbeat_seconds on 2026-09-14 after an audit found phone_status had absorbed 152,773 single-row UPDATEs (180/hour -- more traffic than every other client in this project combined). heartbeat_seconds stays 20 because the LOCAL dark-detection math depends on it beating against the 25s WireGuard Persistent Keepalive (see _dark_buffer above); the SERVER never needed that granularity -- eg_check_phone() only ever asks whether monitor_beat is older than 5 minutes. 60s keeps monitor_beat comfortably fresh against that 5-minute threshold while cutting reports ~3x, and any alive<->dark state change still reports INSTANTLY rather than waiting for the next tick, so alerting is MORE responsive than before, not less.",
  "report_seconds": 60,

  "_router_check": "Router config tamper-evidence: periodically checks that AdGuard protection, Block-DoT, WAN default-deny, SSH key-only auth, GoodCloud staying disabled, the admin GUI staying HTTPS-only, and the WireGuard peer set all still match the hardened state -- flags a RED tamper alert on any drift. The scalar checks compare directly against the fixed expected value every cycle (admin-trust pivot, 2026-08-24) -- NOT gated on the local baseline file also agreeing, since that file is exactly as editable as the config it's meant to catch drift in once SSH is in play. wg_peers still diffs against the local baseline -- a known, accepted residual (the peer set legitimately changes over time, unlike the fixed invariants).",
  "router_check_seconds": 300,
  "router_baseline_file": "/etc/eyeguard/router_baseline.json",

  "_sleep_relay": "LAN relay for the Mac's WillSleep signal (2026-09-04) -- see eyeguard-phone.py's sleep_relay_loop() and eyeguard/session_watcher.py's SleepWatcher for the full reasoning. Set sleep_relay_token to any random string (must match config.yaml's session_watcher.sleep_relay_token on the Mac exactly) to enable; leave blank/omit to disable the listener entirely (the Mac's direct WAN attempt still works on its own either way, same as before this existed). Not internet-facing -- only reachable from the LAN.",
  "sleep_relay_token": "",
  "sleep_relay_port": 51900,

  "_router_watcher": "A SEPARATE process (eyeguard-router-watcher.py, its own service) that verifies eyeguard-phone.py's on-disk hash against a manifest published server-side, and that the process is actually running -- catches the case where eyeguard-phone.py itself was edited to disable its own checks, which nothing INSIDE that same script could ever detect. router_script_version must match a version key already published via deploy/publish_router_manifest.sh.",
  "router_script_version": "unknown",
  "router_watcher_check_seconds": 300,

  "_tor": "Tor bypass detection: DNS-based classify() is blind to Tor by design (it resolves sites inside the encrypted circuit), so this matches outbound TCP:443 SYNs against the current public Guard-relay IP set (Tor's own Onionoo directory API) -- a client's first hop must be Guard-flagged. Refreshed periodically since guard relays churn; a failed refresh keeps the last-known-good list rather than going blind. Known accepted residual: pluggable transports/bridges (obfs4, meek, snowflake) are designed to evade exactly this kind of IP-list detection -- this catches default Tor Browser/Onion Browser usage, not a bridge-configured evasion.",
  "tor_refresh_seconds": 21600,
  "tor_relay_cache_file": "/etc/eyeguard/tor_relays.json",

